  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ニチニチソウ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8B%E3%83%81%E3%83%8B%E3%83%81%E3%82%BD%E3%82%A6",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109709906,
      "text": "ニチニチソウ は、キョウチクトウ科ニチニチソウ属の一年草。ニチニチカ（日日花）とも呼ばれる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Catharanthus roseus」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catharanthus_roseus",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1357671132,
      "text": "Catharanthus roseus, commonly known as bright eyes, Cape periwinkle, graveyard plant, Madagascar periwinkle, old maid, pink periwinkle, rose periwinkle, is a perennial species of flowering plant in the family Apocynaceae. It is native and endemic to Madagascar, but is grown elsewhere as an ornamental and medicinal plant, and now has a pantropical distribution. It is a source of the drugs vincristine and vinblastine, used to treat cancer. It was formerly included in the genus Vinca as Vinca rosea."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "toxicity": {
      "ja": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ニチニチソウ」の節「毒性」",
        "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8B%E3%83%81%E3%83%8B%E3%83%81%E3%82%BD%E3%82%A6#%E6%AF%92%E6%80%A7",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 109709906,
        "heading": "毒性",
        "text": "ニチニチソウには、「ビンカアルカロイド」と総称される、10種以上のアルカロイドが、全草に含まれる。\nそのうち、ビンクリスチンとビンブラスチンには、細胞分裂阻害作用（チューブリン脱重合による）がある。抵抗剤として用いられるが、脱毛などの副作用・毒性があるので、素人の利用は危険。貪食すると、嘔吐や下痢程度では済まない。\n\n症状 - 中枢神経刺激作用、心機能障害、痙攣、筋肉麻痺、嘔吐。",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Catharanthus roseus」の節「Toxicity」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catharanthus_roseus#Toxicity",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1357671132,
        "heading": "Toxicity",
        "text": "C. roseus can be extremely toxic if consumed orally by humans, and is cited (under its synonym Vinca rosea) in the Louisiana State Act 159. All parts of the plant are poisonous. On consumption, symptoms consist of mild stomach cramps, cardiac complications, hypotension, systematic paralysis eventually leading to death.\nAccording to French botanist Pierre Boiteau, its poisonous properties are made known along generations of Malagasy people as a poison consumed in ordeal trials, even before the tangena fruit was used. This lent the flower one of its names vonenina, from Malagasy: vony nenina meaning \"flower of remorse\".",
        "truncated": false
      }
    },
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Catharanthus roseus」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catharanthus_roseus#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1357671132,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "As an ornamental plant, it is appreciated for its hardiness in dry and nutritionally deficient conditions, popular in subtropical gardens where temperatures never fall below 5–7 °C (41–45 °F), and as a warm season bedding plant in temperate gardens. It is noted for its long flowering period, throughout the year in tropical conditions, and from spring to late autumn, in warm temperate climates. Full sun and well-drained soil are preferred. Numerous cultivars have been selected, for variation in flower color (white, mauve, peach, scarlet, and reddish orange), and also for tolerance of cooler growing conditions in temperate regions.\nNotable cultivars include 'Albus' (white flowers), 'Grape Cooler' (rose-pink; cool-tolerant), the Ocellatus Group (various colors), and 'Peppermint Cooler' (white with a red center; cool-tolerant).\nIn the U.S. it often remains identified as \"Vinca\" although botanists have shifted its identification and it often can be seen growing along roadsides in the south.\nIn the United Kingdom it has gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit (confirmed 2017).",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
